The restricted graph $y=\tan x$ is shown. What are the domain and range of $y=\arctan x$?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.5cm, y=0.7cm]
\draw[->] (-2.1,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.9) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (1.5708,-3.7) -- (1.5708,3.7);
\draw[dashed] (-1.5708,-3.7) -- (-1.5708,3.7);
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-1.2925:1.2925,samples=90,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({\t},{tan(deg(\t))});
\node[font=\small] at (1.5708,4.15) {$x=\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\node[font=\small] at (-1.5708,4.15) {$x=-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\node[font=\small] at (0.95,-2.6) {$y=\tan x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The point $\left(\frac{\pi}{3},\frac{1}{2}\right)$ is marked on the restricted graph $y=\cos x$. Which point lies on $y=\arccos x$?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.25]
\draw[->] (-0.8,0) -- (3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.6) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=0:3.1416,samples=80,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({\t},{cos(deg(\t))});
\fill (0,1) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (3.1416,-1) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (1.0472,0.5) circle (2pt);
\node[above right,font=\small] at (1.0472,0.5) {$\left(\frac{\pi}{3},\frac{1}{2}\right)$};
\draw (3.1416,0.07) -- (3.1416,-0.07);
\node[below,font=\small] at (3.1416,-0.07) {$\pi$};
\draw (0.07,-1) -- (-0.07,-1);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.1,-1) {$-1$};
\node[font=\small] at (2.4,-1.35) {$y=\cos x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Which statement about the graph shown is correct?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.4]
\draw[->] (-1.8,0) -- (1.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=-1.5708:1.5708,samples=80,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({sin(deg(\t))},{\t});
\fill (1,1.5708) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (-1,-1.5708) circle (1.6pt);
\draw (1,0.07) -- (1,-0.07);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1,-0.07) {$1$};
\draw (-1,0.07) -- (-1,-0.07);
\node[below,font=\small] at (-1,-0.07) {$-1$};
\draw (0.07,1.5708) -- (-0.07,1.5708);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.1,1.5708) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw (0.07,-1.5708) -- (-0.07,-1.5708);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.1,-1.5708) {$-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\node[font=\small] at (-0.95,1.95) {$y=\arcsin x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

What is the equation of the curve shown?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.4]
\draw[->] (-1.8,0) -- (1.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.0) -- (0,2.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=-1.5708:1.5708,samples=80,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({sin(deg(\t))},{\t});
\fill (1,1.5708) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (-1,-1.5708) circle (1.6pt);
\draw (1,0.07) -- (1,-0.07);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1,-0.07) {$1$};
\draw (-1,0.07) -- (-1,-0.07);
\node[below,font=\small] at (-1,-0.07) {$-1$};
\draw (0.07,1.5708) -- (-0.07,1.5708);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.1,1.5708) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw (0.07,-1.5708) -- (-0.07,-1.5708);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.1,-1.5708) {$-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The restricted graph $y=\sin x$ shown below is reflected in the line $y=x$. Which statement describes the resulting curve?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.4]
\draw[->] (-2.1,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.6) -- (0,1.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (-1.55,-1.55) -- (1.55,1.55);
\draw[thick,domain=-1.5708:1.5708,samples=80,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({\t},{sin(deg(\t))});
\fill (1.5708,1) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (-1.5708,-1) circle (1.6pt);
\node[font=\small] at (-0.95,1.4) {$y=\sin x$};
\node[font=\small] at (1.35,-1.35) {$y=x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The restricted graph $y=\tan x$ shown has vertical asymptotes $x=\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $x=-\frac{\pi}{2}$. After reflection in $y=x$, what do these two lines become?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.5cm, y=0.7cm]
\draw[->] (-2.1,0) -- (2.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.9) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (1.5708,-3.7) -- (1.5708,3.7);
\draw[dashed] (-1.5708,-3.7) -- (-1.5708,3.7);
\draw[thick,<->,domain=-1.2925:1.2925,samples=90,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({\t},{tan(deg(\t))});
\draw[dashed] (-1.4,-1.4) -- (1.4,1.4);
\node[font=\small] at (1.5708,4.15) {$x=\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\node[font=\small] at (-1.5708,4.15) {$x=-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\node[font=\small] at (0.95,-2.6) {$y=\tan x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The graphs of $y=\arcsin x$ and $y=\arccos x$ are shown on the same axes. What is the exact $x$-coordinate of the point where they cross?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=1.3cm, y=0.95cm]
\draw[->] (-2.0,0) -- (2.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.1) -- (0,3.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=-1.5708:1.5708,samples=80,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({sin(deg(\t))},{\t});
\draw[thick,blue,dashed,domain=0:3.1416,samples=80,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({cos(deg(\t))},{\t});
\fill (1,1.5708) circle (1.6pt);
\fill (-1,-1.5708) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[blue] (1,0) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[blue] (-1,3.1416) circle (1.6pt);
\draw (1,0.09) -- (1,-0.09);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1,-0.09) {$1$};
\draw (-1,0.09) -- (-1,-0.09);
\node[below,font=\small] at (-1,-0.09) {$-1$};
\draw (0.07,1.5708) -- (-0.07,1.5708);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.1,1.5708) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw (0.07,3.1416) -- (-0.07,3.1416);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.1,3.1416) {$\pi$};
\node[font=\small] at (-1.15,0.95) {$y=\arcsin x$};
\node[font=\small,blue] at (1.05,2.7) {$y=\arccos x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
